1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,624
You have a pool of disk space. But nobody stores files in a pool.

2
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:10,108
You carve out slices of the right size, and those slices are called logical volumes.

3
00:00:10,578 --> 00:00:13,865
In this video: create and delete logical volumes.

4
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:20,968
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,438 --> 00:00:23,722
Create and delete logical volumes.

6
00:00:24,192 --> 00:00:28,962
We'll start with a volume group, create two logical volumes of different sizes,

7
00:00:28,982 --> 00:00:30,721
look at them, and delete one.

8
00:00:40,241 --> 00:00:42,882
The top layer of LVM: logical volumes.

9
00:00:42,902 --> 00:00:47,645
Each one is a named slice of a volume group, with a size you choose.

10
00:00:47,665 --> 00:00:50,866
You can make as many as the pool has room for.

11
00:00:58,336 --> 00:01:01,446
Our group, vgdata, has about ten gigabytes.

12
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:06,759
We will cut out lvdata, one gigabyte, and lvlogs, five hundred megabytes.

13
00:01:06,779 --> 00:01:08,215
The rest stays free.

14
00:01:16,685 --> 00:01:20,289
The lab already created the group from the two spare disks,

15
00:01:20,309 --> 00:01:23,790
as in the last two videos. It has no logical volumes yet.

16
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:37,474
First, vgs. The group vgdata has two physical volumes, zero logical volumes,

17
00:01:37,494 --> 00:01:43,089
and almost ten gigabytes free. Then lvs, which lists logical volumes.

18
00:01:43,109 --> 00:01:44,879
It prints nothing yet.

19
00:01:55,399 --> 00:01:58,869
Now lvcreate. Dash n gives the name, lvdata.

20
00:01:58,889 --> 00:02:05,373
Dash capital L gives the size, one gigabyte. Last comes the volume group. It says:

21
00:02:05,393 --> 00:02:07,198
logical volume created.

22
00:02:14,668 --> 00:02:18,823
lvs lists it: the name, the group, and the size, one gigabyte.

23
00:02:18,843 --> 00:02:23,672
The long Attr column is a row of flags; the letter a means it is active.

24
00:02:34,192 --> 00:02:36,308
A second one, the same way:

25
00:02:36,328 --> 00:02:42,321
dash n lvlogs, dash capital L, five hundred M, in vgdata. M means megabytes.

26
00:02:48,791 --> 00:02:51,907
lvs now lists both volumes, with their sizes.

27
00:02:58,427 --> 00:03:02,254
Each logical volume gets a device file under slash dev,

28
00:03:02,274 --> 00:03:04,632
in a folder named after the group.

29
00:03:04,652 --> 00:03:09,598
These are links to the real device names, dm dash zero and dm dash one.

30
00:03:09,618 --> 00:03:13,724
This is the path you will format and mount in later videos.

31
00:03:22,194 --> 00:03:24,663
And vgs now shows two logical volumes,

32
00:03:24,683 --> 00:03:27,806
and about eight and a half gigabytes still free.

33
00:03:36,326 --> 00:03:39,762
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

34
00:03:41,282 --> 00:03:44,133
We reboot. Then lvs shows both volumes again:

35
00:03:44,153 --> 00:03:47,769
they are stored on the disks themselves, so they survive.

36
00:03:57,289 --> 00:04:02,139
Now we delete lvlogs. lvremove, dash y, and group slash volume.

37
00:04:02,159 --> 00:04:08,554
Dash y answers yes to the confirmation. Be careful: the data in the volume is gone.

38
00:04:17,024 --> 00:04:18,843
lvs shows only lvdata.

39
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:24,023
And vgs shows one logical volume, and the space is free again.

40
00:04:33,543 --> 00:04:39,622
Your turn. A group called examvg already exists, with an old volume called oldlv.

41
00:04:39,642 --> 00:04:44,969
Create a volume called examlv of three hundred megabytes. Delete oldlv.

42
00:04:44,989 --> 00:04:46,174
Run the checker.

43
00:04:52,644 --> 00:04:54,394
Try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:59,914 --> 00:05:03,926
Next up: mount file systems at boot by UUID or label.

45
00:05:04,396 --> 00:05:07,299
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
